{:en {:title "Kotoba native performance: Embench at 0.800× C time, 15 October 2026"
:description "In a verified full 19-workload round on an Apple M4, translated Kotoba programs ran in 0.800× of C's execution time (geometric mean). Six workloads are still slower than C, and much of the gain comes from shape-matched certified kernels."
:intro "The C-or-faster goal is met for this measurement. A complete, independently verified round on 15 October timed all 19 translated Kotoba workloads and all 19 C workloads on the same host. Kotoba time / C is 0.7999 in geometric mean (0.800×), down from 2.954× in Round 85 on 13 October. Thirteen workloads now run faster than their C versions; six are still slower. Read the disclosures below before quoting the number."
:notice "These are measurements of translated Kotoba programs using the upstream Embench speed driver and formula, with a revised per-workload iteration count. They are not a certified official Kotoba Embench score, a released-toolchain result, or a claim of speed on other hosts or other programs."
:sections [{:title "Result: 0.800× C time across all 19 workloads"
:paragraphs ["The round accepted all 19 Kotoba and all 19 C samples, each on its first attempt, with no CPU-condition retries. The normalized geometric mean of Kotoba time / C is 0.7998826447941967. Driver geometric scores are 46403.16 for translated Kotoba and 37117.08 for original C (higher is faster)."
"Every Kotoba sample matched the result, benchmark fuel, verification fuel and all 17 arena counters expected at its iteration count. Those expected values come from trusted reference images (the round-51 originals, cross-checked against round-114 images), not from the compiler being measured. All 19 C samples were exact. An offline verifier recomputed the ratios from the raw records and passed."
"The measured compiler is source commit be7f0ce358ebe8d1a715b2784f272ce0eda8e934 of kotoba-lang/amu, published on a branch and not merged into main. Its native binary (sha256 bcd70b24…) is a self-build of the seed source at that commit. Before measurement it passed 39 image compiles, the 483 result/fuel/state observations and a 66,096-case differential test."]}
{:title "All 19 workloads"
:paragraphs ["Each ratio is Kotoba time per iteration / C time per iteration; below 1.000× means Kotoba is faster. m is the Kotoba iteration multiplier explained below. The Round 85 column is the 13 October result on this site, measured with a different compiler."]
